|> Exercise
    !! handle=_isosets_of_xy_new_new_ver_

    If each of these dotted lines...

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_x_plus_y_isosets.svg

    ...is a so-called

    __isoset__

    (also: _isoline_, _contour line_, _isoquant_,
    _isosurface_, _isovalue line_, or _isovalue set_$\rt{0.1}$)
    of the two-variable function

    $$
    f : \rr^2\ra \rr
    $$

    given by

    $$
    f(x, y) = x + y
    $$

    then what are similar

    __isosets__

    (😍) of the two-variable function

    $$
    g : \rr^2\ra \rr
    $$

    given by

    $$
    g(x, y) = xy
    $$

    ...?

    !! [In human terms: draw solutions of the equation

    !! $$
    !! xy = C
    !! $$

    !! in $\rr^2$ for some different values of
    !! $C \in \rr$.]

    !! Next: 
    Use any geometric insights gleaned from
    these

    __isosets__

    (😍😍😍) to find the maximum and minimum values of

    $$
    \sin\theta{}\cos\theta
    $$

    for $\theta \in \rr$, and specify the set of
    values of $\theta$ for which the maximum and
    minimum are attained.

    |> Solution

        The isosets of

        $$
        (x, y) \ra xy
        $$

        (lambda-notation for a two-variable function)
        have this general aspect:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_xy_isosets.svg

        Note that each isoset consists of the union of
        TWO disjoint curves, modulo the isoset

        $$
        xy = 0
        $$

        (or: “modulo the isoset

        $$
        \{(x, y) \in \rr^2 : xy = 0 \}
        $$

        ...” to be explicit about the fact that
        the isoset is _a set of points in the plane_),
        that is the union of the $x$- and $y$- axes, which
        intersect. (Hence, not “disjoint”.)

        If we draw a “heatmap” of

        $$
        xy
        $$

        in some region of the plane, 
        the larger (more positive)
        values of $xy$ show up in the first and third
        quadrants, while the smaller (more negative)
        values of $xy$ show up in the second and fourth
        quadrants:
        !! , with boundaries drawn by these isosets:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_rainbow.svg

        At any given
        distance from the origin, the line

        $$
        x = y
        $$

        can be seen seen to hold the largest of values of $xy$,
        while, symmetrically,

        $$
        x = -y
        $$

        can be seen to hold the smallest values:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_fixed_distance.svg

        In particular,

        $$
        \cos\theta\,\sin\theta
        $$

        will reach its maximum at those values of
        $\theta$ for which 

        $$
        (\cos \theta, \sin \theta) = \pm(\sqrt{0.5}, \sqrt{0.5})
        $$

        and will reach its mimumim at those values
        of $\theta$ for which

        $$
        (\cos \theta, \sin \theta) = \pm(-\sqrt{0.5}, \sqrt{0.5})
        $$

        the former values of $\theta$ being all $\theta$
        in the set

        !! $$
        !! \{45^\circ + k\cdot 180^\circ: k \in \zz\}
        !! $$
        $$
        \{0.5\eta + 2\eta k: k \in \zz\}
        $$

        and the latter values of $\theta$ being all $\theta$
        in the set

        $$
        \{1.5\eta + 2\eta k: k \in \zz\}
        $$

        as illustrated here:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_angles_v2.svg

        !! (Recall that

        !! $$
        !! \large
        !! \{\,\,\ldots\,\,\,:\,\,\,\rt{0.1}\ldots\,\}
        !! $$

        !! means “the set of values of the form $\ldots$ such that $\ldots$”, and that

        !! $$
        !! \large
        !! \zz = \{\ldots, -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, \ldots \}
        !! $$

        !! is the set of integers.) 
        The actual maximum and minimum
        _values_ of

        $$
        \cos\theta\,\sin\theta
        $$

        are

        $$
        \sqrt{0.5} \cdot \sqrt{0.5} = (-\sqrt{0.5}) \cdot (-\sqrt{0.5}) = {1\over 2}
        $$

        for the maximum, and

        $$
        (-\sqrt{0.5}) \cdot \sqrt{0.5} = \sqrt{0.5} \cdot (-\sqrt{0.5}) = -{1\over 2}
        $$

        for the minimum, as obtained just by taking
        the product of the $x$- and $y$-coordinates at the points
        of interest.

        |> SolutionNote

            For interest, here is the graph of $x \ra \sin x \cos x$,
            as previously given in Exercise >>_four_graphs_, annotated
            with the newly gleaned information:

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_graph_v2.svg

